Function Result Use <code>kaggle.load_dataset(dataset_name, file_name="")</code> PARDOS DataFrame Read a CSV with headers. <code>kaggle.load_dataset_split(dataset_name, file_name, split="")</code> PARDOS DataFrame Select a file using <code>train</code>, <code>test</code>, or a path. <code>kaggle.load_dataset_matrix(dataset_name, columns, split="", limit=0)</code> <code>List[List[FLOAT]]</code> Read selected numeric columns without creating a DataFrame. <pre><code>import kaggle;
import machine;

List[STR] columns = ["SepalLengthCm", "PetalLengthCm"];
List[List[FLOAT]] data = kaggle.load_dataset_matrix("uciml/iris", columns, "Iris.csv", 50);
MACHINE model = machine.kmeans(3);
model.fit(data);
</code></pre> <p><code>columns</code> selects features. <code>limit=0</code> means all rows. When <code>columns</code> is empty, KAFE infers numeric columns from the first data row. Empty CSV cells become NaN. Other cells are converted to a number when possible. Files are parsed with Python's standard <code>csv</code> library, without pandas.</p>

Import name Library Purpose <code>numk</code> KafeNUMK NumPy-style linear algebra <code>math</code> KafeMATH Mathematical functions <code>plot</code> KafePLOT Data visualization (SVG) <code>files</code> KafeFILES File I/O <code>gesha</code> KafeGESHA Deep learning and neural networks <code>pardos</code> KafePARDOS DataFrames and CSV <code>machine</code> KafeMACHINE ML utilities <pre><code>import numk;
import math;
import plot;
import files;
import gesha;
import pardos;
import machine;
</code></pre>

Syntax Description <code>ID = expression;</code> Simple assignment <code>ID[idx] = expression;</code> Indexed assignment (for List) <pre><code>INT x = 10;
x = x + 5;           -- Reassignment

List[INT] L = [1, 2];
L[0] = 99;           -- Indexed assignment
